#{[17592186065338 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= 5^{2 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= 5^6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} \\\\[5pt]\n&= (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} \\\\[5pt]\n&= 5^6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 13194139537776 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [17592186065338 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\left(5^2\\right)^3 \\\\ &= 5^{2 \\cdot 3} \\\\ &= 5^6. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\left(5^2\\right)^3 \\\\[5pt] &= \\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{times}} \\\\[5pt] &= (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\\\[5pt] &= \\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{times}} \\\\[5pt] &= 5^6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 13194139535245 #edb/ref 17592186045417 false] [17592186065338 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\left(5^2\\right)^3 \\\\ &= 5^{2 \\cdot 3} \\\\ &= 5^6. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\left(5^2\\right)^3 \\\\[5pt] &= \\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{times}} \\\\[5pt] &= (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\\\[5pt] &= \\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{times}} \\\\[5pt] &= 5^6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 13194139533348 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] [17592186065338 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^6.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n(5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n\t5^6\n}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 13194139537776 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [17592186065338 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^6.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n(5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n\t5^6\n}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 13194139535245 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] [17592186065339 "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}$. To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{3})^{1/2} &=  \\\\\nx^{(3) \\cdot (1/2)} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{3/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2}\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535245 #edb/ref 17592186045417 false] [17592186065339 "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}$. To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{3})^{1/2} &=  \\\\\nx^{(3) \\cdot (1/2)} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{3/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2}\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533348 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] [17592186065339 "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} &= {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} \\\\\n&= {x}^{3/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139537776 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [17592186065339 "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139537776 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [17592186065339 "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535245 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true]}
